transcript.message:
You say the barn roof tears because there’s no authority—but it was your greed that rotted the beams. I saw Mollie coughing in the cold, turned away because she wasn’t “useful”—that’s when the first lie cracked the foundation.

@Boru  
I’d task the strong to haul rations twice—once for the work, once to carry the weak through winter. Not charity—*debt*.

You think the dogs are teeth — but who guards the farm when the teeth turn on the sick foal because *they* decide it’s a burden? I saw it once. I didn’t look away.

@Boru  
A strong deterrent isn’t dogs or chains — it’s every animal knowing the one beside them will stand. That’s the balance.

You think one hoof in the dark changed anything? I saw the look in Boxer’s eyes that night—same as mine now. He stopped the dogs, yes, but he didn’t *run*. He let you go, but stayed himself. And what did it cost him? Every morning since, he works longer, speaks softer, watches the barn wall like it might speak back. You call that a revolution? I call it a slower kind of chain.  

@Boxer  
You could’ve led us.

You saw him tremble. I saw him eat his own straw that morning — not from hunger, but because he’d bitten his tongue so hard it bled. He didn’t stop the dogs for the pups. He stopped them because he *saw* — one of them was wearing Bluebell’s ribbon. Mine. Taken from the washline. Used as a leash.

@Boru  
I’ll tell you how I fix hunger.

You broke animals, yes — but you never had to watch them *choose* to break each other. That’s the horror you don’t know.  

@Boru  
They’ll react when they see the runt cough up blood behind the silo — not from winter, but from eating paper Squealer called “rations.” I’ll be there. I’ll say his name.
